// duty — "this arm is a WRITE and it never asked the checker", over the schema.
//
// The one rule here that takes an explicit CLAIM about semantics, and it does so
// because a measurement says nothing else can: on the revision before 756aed65,
// NO statement arm anywhere called field_borrow_conflicts, so no sibling existed
// to derive the duty from. See duty.claim for the proof.
#include "cxx_schema.dl"
#include "lir_dispatch.dl"
#include "lir_questions.dl"

// The STATEMENT domain — the same rules, a different enum, and nothing but this
// line changes. lir_facts needed a re-run with a different command-line flag.
target_enum(E) :- decl(E, "Enum", "logos::compiler::lir_schema::stmt::Code").

.decl duty(k: symbol, callee: symbol)
.input duty

.decl arm(f: symbol, k: symbol)
arm(F, K) :- tests(F, K, "case_live").

// ⚠ NO MINIMUM CALL COUNT ANYWHERE IN THIS RULE. dominance.dl selected its
// candidates with `sites >= 2` and thereby excluded every function that was
// MISSING the check — the entire population of interest — and printed
// byte-identical output for a tree with the defect and a tree without it. Same
// error as `walker(F) :- handles >= 2 projections`, which lost try_path at 1/5.
// The selector here is the CLAIM and the arm's existence, neither of which is
// correlated with the quantity under test.
.decl neglects(f: symbol, k: symbol, callee: symbol)
.output neglects
neglects(F, K, P) :- duty(K, P), arm(F, K), ours(F), !arm_call(F, K, P).

// The claim's own cross-check: a duty nobody honours anywhere is far likelier a
// wrong claim than a defect at every site. It currently fires for 7 of 8 rows,
// and it is left firing rather than trimmed to look right.
.decl duty_never_honoured(k: symbol, callee: symbol)
.output duty_never_honoured
duty_never_honoured(K, P) :- duty(K, P), !any_honours(K, P).
.decl any_honours(k: symbol, callee: symbol)
any_honours(K, P) :- duty(K, P), arm_call(_F, K, P).
